WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.487
This is a practical walkthrough, not a real customer project.

00:00:04.487 --> 00:00:06.287
The repo is synthetic.

00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:08.087
The bug is synthetic.

00:00:08.087 --> 00:00:10.146
The artifacts are synthetic.

00:00:10.146 --> 00:00:11.946
That is on purpose.

00:00:11.946 --> 00:00:23.862
We are going to show the shape of a Bubbles workflow from setup to actual result: commands, responses, generated artifacts, tests, validation, and final evidence.

00:00:23.862 --> 00:00:32.910
The example is a tiny product called pocket CRM demo, where the email retry button spins forever if the provider times out.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.710
Small bug.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.410
Realistic pain.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:36.410
Perfect little clipboard exercise.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.471
We start with a small repo.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.914
Not an enterprise space station.

00:00:40.914 --> 00:00:44.044
Just a web UI, an API, and a test folder.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:48.395
The product sends follow-up emails from a contact record.

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:55.418
The bug is simple: if the email provider times out, the retry button keeps spinning forever.

00:00:55.418 --> 00:00:57.861
Users do not see a clear status.

00:00:57.861 --> 00:01:00.457
Sometimes the action queues twice.

00:01:00.457 --> 00:01:09.007
The desired result is also simple: the button recovers, the status is visible, and retry is queued exactly once.

00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:10.807
Notice the phrasing.

00:01:10.807 --> 00:01:16.030
We are already thinking in user-visible behavior, not just code changes.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.982
Next, install and bootstrap.

00:01:17.982 --> 00:01:27.464
The exact install command depends on how you distribute Bubbles in your environment, so this panel shows the shape, not a live download.

00:01:27.464 --> 00:01:37.295
After setup, the repo has the local Bubbles layer: agents, prompts, instructions, skills, templates, workflow scripts, and governance checks.

00:01:37.295 --> 00:01:39.095
Then run doctor.

00:01:39.095 --> 00:01:41.953
The important habit is boring but useful.

00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:49.344
Before asking the framework to guide real work, confirm that the framework surface is present and healthy.

00:01:49.344 --> 00:01:53.400
Exciting setup usually means somebody is about to lose an afternoon.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.230
If you do not know which route to take, ask super.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:05.961
Super is the first-touch assistant for Bubbles questions, agent selection, commands, recipes, setup, and recovery.

00:02:05.961 --> 00:02:12.011
In this synthetic example, we say: fix the retry spinner bug, and I need proof.

00:02:12.011 --> 00:02:20.896
Super recommends slash bubbles dot workflow, with a bugfix fastlane style path, because this is not just a question.

00:02:20.896 --> 00:02:25.338
We need artifacts, a fix, tests, validation, and evidence.

00:02:25.338 --> 00:02:28.172
That is bigger than one quick review.

00:02:28.172 --> 00:02:30.240
That is workflow territory.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.748
Now run workflow with the actual user problem.

00:02:33.748 --> 00:02:36.722
Do not start with, change this handler.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.553
Start with the behavior.

00:02:38.553 --> 00:02:41.603
Email retry spins forever after timeout.

00:02:41.603 --> 00:02:45.416
Expected result: recover, show status, queue once.

00:02:45.416 --> 00:02:51.746
The workflow resolves a bugfix route and creates a synthetic feature or bug folder.

00:02:51.746 --> 00:02:56.475
Then it starts in the right order: analysis, design, planning.

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:58.275
That is the point.

00:02:58.275 --> 00:03:04.940
Bubbles tries to understand what must be true before it starts flinging code around the park.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.218
The first important artifact is the spec.

00:03:08.218 --> 00:03:13.416
In this synthetic example, the spec captures an outcome contract.

00:03:13.416 --> 00:03:16.374
Intent: retry recovers after timeout.

00:03:16.374 --> 00:03:19.733
Success signal: the user sees queued once.

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:22.931
Hard constraint: no duplicate retry job.

00:03:22.931 --> 00:03:26.849
Failure condition: spinner remains after timeout.

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:30.127
That is more useful than, fix the button.

00:03:30.127 --> 00:03:35.245
It gives tests and implementation something concrete to satisfy.

00:03:35.245 --> 00:03:38.763
Bubbles wants requirements to be observable.

00:03:38.763 --> 00:03:44.760
If nobody can prove the outcome, the outcome is probably fog wearing a hat.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.713
Next, design names the safe fix shape.

00:03:47.713 --> 00:03:56.495
This is where the workflow stops the agent from doing the classic thing: hiding the spinner and calling it fixed.

00:03:56.495 --> 00:04:02.091
The design identifies the timeout boundary around the email client call.

00:04:02.091 --> 00:04:10.174
It defines idempotency using contact ID and template ID, so repeated clicks do not queue duplicate jobs.

00:04:10.174 --> 00:04:16.003
It names UI state transitions: sending, timeout, retryable, queued, failed.

00:04:16.003 --> 00:04:18.567
Now the implementation has rails.

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:20.367
It is not just a patch.

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:23.930
It is a behavior change with failure handling.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.371
Then plan slices the work into proveable scopes.

00:04:27.371 --> 00:04:30.382
Scope one might be retry timeout recovery.

00:04:30.382 --> 00:04:37.765
It names the scenario, the API test, the UI test, the files likely touched, and the definition of done.

00:04:37.765 --> 00:04:40.633
This is where Bubbles gets strict again.

00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:45.006
Every test-plan row needs a matching definition-of-done item.

00:04:45.006 --> 00:04:49.593
Later, every checked definition-of-done item needs raw evidence.

00:04:49.593 --> 00:04:52.317
That means the plan is not decorative.

00:04:52.317 --> 00:04:55.830
It is a contract for how the work will be proven.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.847
Now implementation can change behavior.

00:04:58.847 --> 00:05:05.499
In this synthetic diff, the API returns a retryable state when the provider times out.

00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:08.593
The retry queue uses an idempotency key.

00:05:08.593 --> 00:05:15.400
The UI stops the spinner, shows retry available, and confirms queued status after retry.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:23.755
Notice what is not happening: the agent is not only changing CSS, and it is not only changing an API branch.

00:05:23.755 --> 00:05:25.843
The vertical slice matters.

00:05:25.843 --> 00:05:30.794
The user-visible fix crosses UI, API, queue behavior, and tests.

00:05:30.794 --> 00:05:36.750
This is exactly where Bubbles earns its keep: keeping the whole path in view.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.486
Then run tests that can actually fail.

00:05:39.486 --> 00:05:44.454
This is not the time for a happy provider response and a victory lap.

00:05:44.454 --> 00:05:47.478
The regression case simulates the timeout.

00:05:47.478 --> 00:05:52.158
The API test asserts that duplicate retry attempts queue one job.

00:05:52.158 --> 00:05:58.638
The UI test asserts the spinner stops after timeout and queued status appears after retry.

00:05:58.638 --> 00:06:02.598
If the old bug comes back, these tests should get loud.

00:06:02.598 --> 00:06:04.542
Quiet tests are not polite.

00:06:04.542 --> 00:06:05.910
They are expensive.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:07.850
The report records evidence.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.622
Not a summary pretending to be evidence.

00:06:10.622 --> 00:06:15.957
The command, the observed output, the pass count, and the behavior assertion.

00:06:15.957 --> 00:06:18.451
This is what a reviewer needs later.

00:06:18.451 --> 00:06:22.747
It is also what the next agent needs after context compaction.

00:06:22.747 --> 00:06:31.893
When Bubbles says raw evidence, this is the spirit: show what ran, show what passed, and show what user-visible behavior was proven.

00:06:31.893 --> 00:06:36.050
Anything less is just trust me bro with Markdown formatting.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.297
Now validation gates push back.

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:42.864
This is the part that makes Bubbles feel strict, and it should.

00:06:42.864 --> 00:06:48.880
State-transition guard checks whether the work is even allowed to move toward done.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:54.896
Artifact lint checks structure, checkboxes, evidence sections, and required fields.

00:06:54.896 --> 00:06:58.738
Reality scan looks for stubs and fake implementation.

00:06:58.738 --> 00:07:01.565
Integration completeness checks wiring.

00:07:01.565 --> 00:07:06.204
Vertical slice completeness checks frontend-to-backend behavior.

00:07:06.204 --> 00:07:10.409
If something is missing, the gate fails and routes rework.

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:12.209
That is not bureaucracy.

00:07:12.209 --> 00:07:15.700
That is the system refusing to publish fake done.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.513
After the fix works, the quality agents add pressure.

00:07:19.513 --> 00:07:24.046
Chaos tries real-system usage variations around the retry path.

00:07:24.046 --> 00:07:25.846
Double click.

00:07:25.846 --> 00:07:27.646
Navigate away.

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:29.446
Return.

00:07:29.446 --> 00:07:31.246
Retry after timeout.

00:07:31.246 --> 00:07:35.347
Simplify reviews the diff and cleans awkward duplication.

00:07:35.347 --> 00:07:37.937
Harden checks completion and policy.

00:07:37.937 --> 00:07:43.189
Security looks for auth, exposure, and risk issues around the retry path.

00:07:43.189 --> 00:07:53.040
This is how Bubbles avoids the classic delivery pattern where the first implementation technically works, and the second developer quietly inherits a shed full of questionable wiring.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.733
Docs and specs need to stay in sync.

00:07:55.733 --> 00:08:01.268
The fix is not complete if durable truth still describes the old behavior.

00:08:01.268 --> 00:08:07.102
In the synthetic response, docs now explain timeout recovery and retry status.

00:08:07.102 --> 00:08:11.141
The spec outcome contract reflects the final behavior.

00:08:11.141 --> 00:08:14.806
Spec-review confirms the active truth is current.

00:08:14.806 --> 00:08:17.349
State records the completed scope.

00:08:17.349 --> 00:08:19.149
This is not glamorous.

00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:24.380
It is how future work starts from reality instead of historical fiction.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.193
The actual result is not, code changed.

00:08:27.193 --> 00:08:30.367
The actual result is verified user behavior.

00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:32.748
Timeout leads to retry available.

00:08:32.748 --> 00:08:34.840
Retry leads to queued status.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:39.241
Repeated clicks lead to one queue job, not a tiny job parade.

00:08:39.241 --> 00:08:44.867
The artifacts show what changed, what ran, what passed, and what risk remains.

00:08:44.867 --> 00:08:49.412
The final answer can be concise because the proof trail exists.

00:08:49.412 --> 00:08:59.800
That is the practical Bubbles loop: setup, route, artifacts, implementation, tests, gates, quality pressure, docs, and a result you can inspect.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.808
Use this pattern on one real workflow.

00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:10.803
Pick a painful bug, a drifted spec, or a feature where you need more than a cheerful final paragraph.

00:09:10.803 --> 00:09:13.653
Start with the user-visible outcome.

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:15.790
Let Bubbles route the work.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.590
Inspect the artifacts.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.490
Run the tests and gates.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:24.477
Use the quality agents when the blast radius deserves pressure.

00:09:24.477 --> 00:09:26.694
Keep docs and specs current.

00:09:26.694 --> 00:09:29.385
Then judge the result by evidence.

00:09:29.385 --> 00:09:31.185
Not charm.

00:09:31.185 --> 00:09:32.985
Not vibes.

00:09:32.985 --> 00:09:33.760
Evidence.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:33.760
That is the practical move.
